Rast wrote:
> Furthermore, if you have reflection, you don't need blindness to protect
> yourself from Medusa's stoning attack (but why are you confronting her?
> Desperate for lev boots?).

Making sure you've got a clear path for the return trip?  I mean, you
*are* planning on coming back up, right?

> > The Castle
>
> My experience is that usually I am too weak (AC, HP, weapon, intrinsics)
> to bull through the monsters, so I need a "trick":

Had fun last night standing a knight's move from the portculis and
casting skilled fireball as far in as I could see--took out most of the
monsters, including two liches and a demi-lich.  Had a relatively weak
monster (ok, I think it was an earth elemental, actually) standing on
the drawbridge poking at me while I did that, but he never took off
more than 5-10 HP at most.  (Level 15 wizard)

> - conflict
> - chicken
> - high level pet(s) + magic whistle
> - Eye + good support spells (extra healing, sleep) or magic missile
> - levitate around the back and let monsters fall down
> - drawbridge

So, yeah, my wizard had the Eye.  :-)  Letting the monsters fall down
into the Valley is foolish--you've got a natural chokepoint in the form
of the back hallway, so you only have to fight one at a time (plus
anything that gets thrown); if they drop down to the Valley, you get
swarmed when you finally follow them.  I also dislike using the
drawbridge because it destroys the loot.

> > Rodney's Tower
>
> If you aren't already wearing conflict, now is a good time to put it on.
> Don't remove it until you reach your temple on Astral.  The other ring
> slot should default to free action, but switch to levitation or TC as
> needed.

Conflict's useful, but not *that* useful.  Put it on when there are
more enemies on screen than you want to deal with, but when you're
running through an empty level, it's just not worth it.  Especially if
you're a chaotic who summoned a demon or two during a sac-fest, and
never bothered to stash them in a safe place.  Rather than getting in
your way, they'd get in your way AND attack you.

> > Moloch's Sanctum
> > There are doors in and out of the graveyard. Use them to fight one
> > monster at a time if your HP levels drop, or if you feel secure,
> > bull through the mob the best way you can.
>
> If your HP/AC are bad enough that the graveyard poses any threat at all,
> the priests and the HPOM are going to destroy you.

Also, be aware that the graveyard monsters start out asleep.  If you
have stealth (and a cursed potion of gain level), you can slip through,
quietly killing at most half a dozen of them, and then lock the door
behind you, at which point it doesn't matter how much noise you make
fighting the priests--the graveyard monsters can't get at you.

> > Plane of Air
> > If engulfed by an Air Elemental, try to take care of it quickly.
> > With speed 36, it is at least twice as fast as you and hits with
> > 1d10 each of its turns.
>
> The danger is that (so far as I can tell) it ignores the damage reduction
> of negative AC.

And that it's very, very fast, and hits hard, just as Maniac said.  :-)

> > Astral Plane
> Of course conflict works fine instead.  There is an excellent chance that
> the Riders, who have low HP, will die before reaching you.  Conflict
> combines well with teleport wands, because instead of immediately filling
> the new gap, monsters will often stop to fight each other.

Displacement, at least, gives pretty much the same benefit (I'm not
sure about invisibility)--in my last ascension, I saw monsters
attacking thin air/each other when they would've otherwise been trying
to fill in the gaps.  I also saw a dragon use his breath attack on a
square next to me, hitting a good number of the monsters in my way.
And I got to keep my pet angel.

Nethack is a game of options and choices; there's never a single right
answer.  Conflict is nice, but it's not anywhere near as necessary as
you make it sound.

